| `import_layers.order` | list of lists of strings | `[["scripts/module_identity.py", "scripts/bounded_match.py", "scripts/child_env.py", "scripts/config_loader.py", "scripts/corpus_level.py", "scripts/exit_codes.py", "scripts/resource_limits.py", "scripts/tool_reports.py"], ["src/shared"], ["src/backend"], ["src/app"], ["models", "runtimes"], ["agents"], ["evals"], ["api", "mcp", "demo"], ["scripts"], ["tests"]]` | a non-empty list of non-empty lists of repo-relative path prefixes, no trailing `/`, each prefix once | `tests/source_tree.py` (`import_layers`); `tests/integration/test_import_layers_gate.py` (`layer_findings`); `tests/integration/test_gate_scope.py` (`_bottom_import_layer`) | Lists the layers bottom to top, and every file of the first layer is held to the oldest interpreter a hook runs. A file sits in the layer of its longest prefix, matched by whole path segments, and an import into a higher layer fails `tests/integration/test_import_layers_gate.py`; so do a file no prefix places and a prefix that is on disk but places no file. A prefix whose path is absent, such as a transport copier pruned, is skipped. | the private-submodule rule, which check_D enforces with no setting | always |
